In multi-agent systems, collaboration is vital as it enables task accomplishment beyond individual capabilities. This study focuses on coordination among self-regulating agents selecting from multiple available options within a stochastic context where agents face binary choices involving randomly varying and partially hidden task complexities. Collaborative efforts allow for splitting difficult tasks into smaller sub-tasks or dividing entire problems into parts. Agent workloads are balanced by collaboratively allocating subsets of a problem among them. We frame this stochastic game as an abstract collection of mathematical functions representing variable difficulty and opacity. Without loss of generality, the found equilibriums are Nash in both continuous and discrete action domains where we provide closed-form solutions for expected payoffs at these equilibriums. Our experimental confirmations extend beyond the simplified models used in verification, showcasing the approaches effectiveness against various frameworks and underlying processes of difficulty/visibility dynamics. The derived equilibriums are robust even when real-world observed patterns of difficulty or opacity deviate from those assumed in the game model.

